## RATE LIMIT ARCHITECTURE (post-suspension)

The suspension was caused by aggressive REST polling. The hardened architecture:

### Layer 1 — Octokit Throttling Plugin
  Uses @octokit/plugin-throttling to enforce:
    onRateLimit: exponential backoff, max 4 retries
    onSecondaryRateLimit: immediate backoff, max 4 retries
  All throttle callbacks write to process.stderr (NOT stdout — stdout corrupts MCP stdio).

### Layer 2 — ETag Conditional Requests
  cache.json (in state repo or local) stores ETag values per endpoint.
  Pattern:
    GET /repos → response has ETag: "abc123"
    Next call: GET /repos + If-None-Match: "abc123"
    If unchanged: 304 Not Modified → FREE (no rate limit cost), use cached response
    If changed: 200 OK + new ETag → update cache

### Layer 3 — GraphQL for Multi-Resource Queries
  Hot REST chains (e.g. list repos → list branches → list alerts per repo)
  replaced with single GraphQL queries.
  GraphQL has a separate 5,000-point budget independent of REST request count.
  POST https://api.github.com/graphql
  Authorization: Bearer <installation-token>

### Layer 4 — Webhook-Driven Architecture (target state)
  Replace all polling with event-driven updates.
  GitHub webhooks → n8n or Cloudflare Worker endpoint → git-steer processes event
  No scheduled polls. Zero idle rate consumption.
  Status: planned, not yet implemented.

### Critical: stdout vs stderr
  ALL logging must go to process.stderr.
  process.stdout is the MCP protocol pipe — ANY output there corrupts JSON-RPC.
  Pattern for logging: process.stderr.write('[git-steer] message\n')
  NEVER: console.log(), console.warn() in MCP server path.
  console.* is only safe in bin/cli.js non-stdio commands (scan, status, reset).

